<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mangareva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mangareva is the largest of the Gambier Islands in the far south-east of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. Its Polynesian people were linked by voyaging with Pitcairn and Henderson islands, and it may have been a stepping stone for the settlers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a>. Catholic missionaries arrived in 1834 and under Father Honoré Laval built stone churches, including the cathedral at Rikitea, while the population fell sharply; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the islands in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mangareva.jpg" alt="Mangareva" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Johnson Space Center - Earth Sciences and Image Analysis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mangareva.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mangareva is the central and largest island of the Gambier Islands in French Polynesia, surrounded by smaller islands: Taravai to the south-west, Aukena and Akamaru to the south-east, and others to the north. It is about eight kilometres long and 15.4 square kilometres in area, some 56 percent of the land of the whole group. A high ridge runs its length, and Mount Duff, at 441 metres, is the highest point of the Gambiers. The island lies within a large lagoon 24 kilometres across, whose reefs gave ancient islanders fish and shellfish and helped them survive much more successfully than on nearby islands without reefs. Rikitea is the chief town of the Gambier Islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> first settled Mangareva in the first millennium CE. Carbon dating has so far dated settlements only to 1160 to 1220, but trade with the Pitcairn Islands from 1000 CE and likely settlement around 800 CE are suggested. The island was once heavily forested and supported a large population that traded by canoe, but logging between the 10th and 15th centuries deforested it, with disastrous effects on environment and economy. The first European visitor was the British captain James Wilson, who arrived in 1797 on the ship Duff and named the group for Admiral James Gambier.</p><p>The islands were ruled by a line of kings and later regents. King Maputeoa asked for a French protectorate on 16 February 1844, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> never ratified; in 1870 the prince regent Arone Teikatoara withdrew the request, but on 30 November 1871 he signed an agreement with the French authority in Tahiti reaffirming the protectorate. The Gambier Islands were annexed on 21 February 1881 under Prince Regent Bernardo Putairi, and the French president approved this on 30 January 1882. In July 1966 the islanders were exposed to radioactive fallout from French nuclear tests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moruroa">Moruroa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fangataufa">Fangataufa</a>; officials knew the doses and kept them secret until 1998, and the French government acknowledged it in 2006.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mangareva has a permanent population of 1,239 in 2012 and is reached by boat from the airport across the lagoon. It is the usual link to Pitcairn Island, a 32-hour boat journey away. Islanders developed a binary number system, reported in 2013, that shortened counting: 150 needs four signs in it, VTPK for 80, 40, 20 and 10, against eight digits in modern binary. Later work in 2020 showed it to be an extension of the traditional Polynesian way of counting objects in pairs, fours and eights. Mangarevan mythology shares gods common across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, among them Tu, the most important god, and the demigod Māui.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mangareva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rapa Nui, the island that Europeans named Easter Island, is a small triangle of volcanic land some 3,700 kilometres off the coast of Chile and about 2,600 kilometres from <b>Mangareva</b> in eastern Polynesia, one of the most remote inhabited places on Earth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
